What solution did you provide?

==============================================================================

ANSWER
==============================================================================

Yes, one of the biggest challenges I faced was during a banking project where
we migrated a large monolithic application into multiple Spring Boot
microservices communicating through Kafka.

Initially everything worked fine in lower environments, but during integration
testing we started seeing intermittent data inconsistencies.

==============================================================================

SITUATION
==============================================================================

Architecture:

Risk Service
     |
Kafka
     |
Control Service
     |
Audit Service

Issue:

Some transactions were successfully processed in one service but were missing
in another service.

Example:

Risk Service      -> APPROVED
Control Service   -> APPROVED
Audit Service     -> No Record

Business users started reporting mismatched data.

==============================================================================

CHALLENGE
==============================================================================

The issue was difficult because:

• Not reproducible 100% of the time
• No obvious application errors
• Multiple microservices involved
• Multiple teams involved
• Thousands of Kafka events processed daily

Initially, I was not very experienced with distributed event-driven systems,
so understanding exactly where the message flow was breaking was challenging.

==============================================================================

WHAT I DID
==============================================================================

Step 1 - Broke Problem Into Smaller Pieces

Instead of investigating the whole system, I traced:

Event ID
      |
Kafka Topic
      |
Kafka Partition
      |
Consumer Logs
      |
Database Records

==============================================================================

Step 2 - Worked Closely With Team
==============================================================================

I consulted:

• Senior Architect
• Kafka SME
• Database Team
• QA Team

Rather than struggling alone, I leveraged team expertise.

==============================================================================

Step 3 - Deep Log Analysis
==============================================================================

Used:

• Splunk
• Kibana
• OpenShift Logs

Tracked:

EventId
RiskId
Kafka Offset
Partition

Discovered:

Some consumers failed during DB updates.

==============================================================================

Step 4 - Reproduced In QA
==============================================================================

We simulated:

• Database downtime
• Network delays
• Kafka consumer failures

After simulation we reproduced the issue.

==============================================================================

ROOT CAUSE
==============================================================================

Consumer processed event.

Database update failed.

No proper retry mechanism existed.

Result:

Event lost from business flow.

==============================================================================

SOLUTION IMPLEMENTED
==============================================================================

I proposed:

1. Retry Mechanism
2. Dead Letter Topic (DLT)
3. Better Logging
4. Monitoring Dashboard
5. Idempotent Processing

Flow:

Event
   |
Consumer
   |
Failure
   |
Retry
   |
Failure Again
   |
DLT

==============================================================================

RESULT
==============================================================================

✔ No Event Loss

✔ Better Traceability

✔ Faster Root Cause Analysis

✔ Improved Reliability

✔ Business Issue Resolved

==============================================================================

WHAT I LEARNED
==============================================================================

1. Never assume logs tell the complete story immediately.

2. Reproducing a production issue in lower environments is critical.

3. Large problems should be broken into smaller traceable components.

4. Collaboration is often faster than working in isolation.

5. Monitoring and observability are as important as coding.

==============================================================================

BEST INTERVIEW ANSWER (2 MINUTES)
==============================================================================

"One significant challenge in my banking project was diagnosing intermittent
data inconsistencies between microservices communicating through Kafka.
Initially, the issue was difficult to reproduce and involved multiple teams.


I started by tracing business transactions using event IDs, partitions,
offsets, and application logs. After collaborating with architects, QA,
and database teams, we reproduced the problem by simulating database failures
in a lower environment. 

We discovered that certain Kafka consumer failures
during database updates were not being handled properly. I helped implement
retry mechanisms, Dead Letter Topics, idempotent processing, and monitoring.


As a result, we eliminated data loss and significantly improved system
reliability. The experience taught me the importance of structured problem
solving, collaboration, and observability in distributed systems."

==============================================================================

ONE-LINE ANSWER
==============================================================================

"My biggest struggle was debugging intermittent Kafka-based data inconsistencies
across microservices, and I overcame it through systematic log analysis,
issue simulation, team collaboration, and implementation of Retry, DLT,
and monitoring solutions."

